@node Numerics
### Subsection Numerics

Numeric slots store integer values and are defined using the PICTURE
clause with 9s for digits. They support arithmetic operations and
can be used in mathematical expressions.

@example
01  counter        PIC 9(4)    VALUE 0.
01  score          PIC 9(6)    VALUE 0.
01  lives-left     PIC 9       VALUE 3.
@end example

@node Arrays of numerics
### Subsection Arrays of numerics

Arrays of numeric values are defined using the OCCURS clause. They
allow storing multiple related values that can be accessed by index.

@example
01  high-scores.
    05  score-entry OCCURS 10 TIMES.
        10  initials  PIC XXX.
        10  points    PIC 9(6).
@end example

@node Character strings
### Subsection Character strings

Character strings store textual data and are defined using the PICTURE
clause with As for alphabetic characters or Xs for alphanumeric characters.

@example
01  player-name    PIC X(20)   VALUE SPACES.
01  level-title    PIC A(30)   VALUE "Level One".
01  message-box    PIC X(80)   VALUE SPACES.
@end example

@node Processing with Skyline-Tool
@section Processing with Skyline-Tool

Before compilation with EIGHTBOL, class definitions often require
processing with Skyline-Tool to resolve dependencies, generate
metadata, and prepare the class hierarchy for use by the various
front-end languages. Skyline-Tool performs several important functions:

@itemize @bullet
@item Resolves inheritance relationships between classes
@item Generates virtual method tables for object-oriented dispatch
@item Computes memory layouts for object instances
@item Produces header files for use with assembly language code
@item Validates slot definitions and detects conflicts
@end itemize

Skyline-Tool is typically invoked as part of the build process before
running EIGHTBOL:

@example
skyline-tool process Classes.Defs
bin/eightbol -l cobol -c 6502 -o game.asm main.cobol
@end example

